          <pane id='1' selection-relaxation-option='selection-relaxation-allow' y-axis-name='[federated.19c4tnk037p8j317kfd620m02dcs].[avg:Production Speed (units/hr):qk]'>
            <view>
              <breakdown value='auto' />
            </view>
            <mark class='Automatic' />
            <customized-tooltip>
              <formatted-text>
                <run fontcolor='#757575' fontsize='9'>Efficiency Status:&#9;</run>
                <run bold='true' fontsize='9'><![CDATA[<[federated.19c4tnk037p8j317kfd620m02dcs].[none:Efficiency_Status:nk]>]]></run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run fontcolor='#757575' fontsize='9'>Avg. Production Speed (units/hr):	</run>
                <run bold='true' fontsize='9'><![CDATA[<[federated.19c4tnk037p8j317kfd620m02dcs].[avg:Production Speed (units/hr):qk]>]]></run>
                <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                <run bold='true' fontsize='9'>Observation:</run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='9'>Production Speed is the sole driver of overall factory Efficiency Status. Because higher speeds do not negatively impact the defect rate (shown above), factory managers can safely push machines to maximum speed without compromising quality.</run>
              </formatted-text>
            </customized-tooltip>
            <style>
              <style-rule element='mark'>
                <format attr='mark-color' value='#4d3dff' />
              </style-rule>
            </style>
          </pane>
          <pane id='2' selection-relaxation-option='selection-relaxation-allow' y-axis-name='[federated.19c4tnk037p8j317kfd620m02dcs].[avg:Quality Control Defect Rate (%):qk]'>
            <view>
              <breakdown value='auto' />
            </view>
            <mark class='Automatic' />
            <customized-tooltip>
              <formatted-text>
                <run fontcolor='#757575' fontsize='9'>Efficiency Status:&#9;</run>
                <run bold='true' fontsize='9'><![CDATA[<[federated.19c4tnk037p8j317kfd620m02dcs].[none:Efficiency_Status:nk]>]]></run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run fontcolor='#757575' fontsize='9'>Avg. Quality Control Defect Rate (%):	</run>
                <run bold='true' fontsize='9'><![CDATA[<[federated.19c4tnk037p8j317kfd620m02dcs].[avg:Quality Control Defect Rate (%):qk]>]]></run>
                <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                <run bold='true' fontsize='9'>Observation:</run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run fontcolor='#000000' fontname='Benton Sans Book' fontsize='9'>The defect rate remains at a flat ~5% baseline across all efficiency levels. This proves that pushing machines to higher production speeds does NOT create a quality bottleneck or cause an increase in manufacturing defects.</run>
              </formatted-text>
